# `let s = #"a \(n) b"#` - a raw string, where `\(` does NOT interpolate.
#
# This is the specimen a byte measurement cannot see. Inside a `#`-delimited
# raw string the interpolation sigil widens to `\#(`, so the `\(n)` here is
# six literal bytes. A hand that reuses the ordinary string interior builds a
# raw_str_interpolation over text that is not one - the right node in the
# wrong place, which reads as full coverage on every board this repo keeps.
roots 1
mends 0
holds raw_string_literal
lacks raw_str_interpolation
spans raw_string_literal 8 20
